<chapter xml:id="ch-state-model">
  <title>State Model</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The previous chapter fixed the <xref ref="gt-item-design" text="custom">item design</xref>:
      the <xref ref="gt-card" text="custom">cards</xref>,
      <xref ref="gt-slot" text="custom">slots</xref>,
      <xref ref="gt-tray" text="custom">tray</xref>,
      <xref ref="gt-key" text="custom">key</xref>, decoys, and
      <xref ref="gt-feedback-policy" text="custom">feedback policy</xref> chosen by
      the author. This chapter defines the corresponding <term>state model</term>.
      A <xref ref="gt-state" text="custom">state</xref> is a snapshot of the
      <xref ref="gt-board" text="custom">board</xref> at one moment. It records
      where every card is, but not how the student arrived there, what
      <xref ref="gt-feedback" text="custom">feedback</xref> the student has seen,
      or how much time passed between <xref ref="gt-action" text="custom">actions</xref>.
      Those process questions belong to the trajectory layer.
    </p>

    <p>
      A state is first a <xref ref="gt-configuration-function" text="custom">configuration
      function</xref> <m>s:
      \mathcal{P}\to\mathcal{Z}</m>, assigning each card to exactly one
      <xref ref="gt-zone" text="custom">zone</xref>. The same
      <xref ref="gt-configuration" text="custom">configuration</xref> can also be
      written as a vector, one zone index per card, or as a
      <xref ref="gt-membership-matrix" text="custom">membership matrix</xref>, a
      zero-one table with one row per zone and one column per card. The sections
      below introduce these representations in order, then define the initial
      and key states, the valid matrix state space, occupancy summaries,
      occupied-zone rank, and the distance between two states.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-configuration-state">
    <title>States and Configurations</title>

    <p>
      A <term>configuration</term> is the card-to-zone content of a board state.
      We keep the notation from the item design: cards are indexed by
      <m>\mathcal{P}=\{1,\dots,P\}</m>, zones by
      <m>\mathcal{Z}=\{0,1,\dots,R\}</m>, zone <m>0</m> is the tray, and zones
      <m>1,\dots,R</m> are the authored slots.
    </p>

    <p>
      Formally, a configuration is a function
      <md>
        s:\mathcal{P}\to\mathcal{Z},
      </md>
      where <m>s(j)</m> is the zone occupied by card <m>j</m>. Equivalently, if
      <m>s(j)=i</m>, then card <m>c_j</m> sits in zone <m>z_i</m>. The key
      configuration <m>s^\star</m> is the particular configuration fixed by the
      author.
    </p>

    <p>
      The terms <term>state</term> and <term>configuration</term> refer to the
      same board snapshot. We use <term>configuration</term> when emphasizing the
      card-to-zone assignment and <term>state</term> when emphasizing that the
      assignment is one possible moment in a student's work.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-configuration-vector">
    <title>Vector Representation</title>

    <p>
      Because the domain <m>\mathcal{P}=\{1,\dots,P\}</m> is finite and ordered,
      a configuration function can be recorded as the vector
      <md xml:id="an-configuration-vector">
        \mathbf{s}=\bigl(s(1),s(2),\dots,s(P)\bigr)
        \in \{0,1,\dots,R\}^P.
      </md>
      This is the most compact representation used here: one zone index per
      card. When no ambiguity is likely, we use the same symbol <m>s</m> for the
      function and for its vector form.
    </p>

    <example>
      <title>Configuration</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For the running example, the key configuration is
          <md>
            s^\star=(1,1,2,0).
          </md>
          Thus
          <ul>
            <li><p><m>c_1</m>, the equilateral triangle, belongs in <m>z_1</m>, the Triangle slot;</p></li>
            <li><p><m>c_2</m>, the right triangle, also belongs in <m>z_1</m>;</p></li>
            <li><p><m>c_3</m>, the square, belongs in <m>z_2</m>, the Quadrilateral slot; and</p></li>
            <li><p><m>c_4</m>, the circle, belongs in <m>z_0</m>, the tray.</p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
          If the circle is placed in the Pentagon zone <m>z_3</m> while the other
          cards remain in their key zones, the resulting configuration is
          <md>
            s=(1,1,2,3).
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <p>
      The vector form is especially useful for storage and replay because changing
      the position of one card changes exactly one coordinate.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-membership-matrix">
    <title>Membership-Matrix Representation</title>

    <p>
      A <xref ref="gt-configuration-vector" text="custom">configuration vector</xref>
      records the zone index of each card. A membership matrix records the same
      information as a table of zeros and ones, with rows indexed by zones and
      columns indexed by cards.
    </p>

    <p>
      Given a configuration <m>s:\mathcal{P}\to\mathcal{Z}</m>, define the
      <term>membership matrix</term> <m>X=X(s)</m> by
      <md xml:id="an-membership-matrix">
        X\in\{0,1\}^{(R+1)\times P},
        \qquad
        X_{ij}
        =
        \begin{cases}
          1, \amp \text{if } s(j)=i,\\
          0, \amp \text{otherwise.}
        \end{cases}
      </md>
      The row index <m>i</m> runs through the zone labels
      <m>0,1,\dots,R</m>, while the column index <m>j</m> runs through the card
      labels <m>1,\dots,P</m>. Thus each row corresponds to a zone and each
      column to a card.
    </p>

    <p>
      Reading down column <m>j</m>, the single <m>1</m> marks the zone occupied
      by card <m>j</m>. In particular, every valid membership matrix has exactly
      one <m>1</m> in each column and is therefore a zero-one column-stochastic
      matrix.
    </p>

    <p>
      Let <m>e_0,e_1,\dots,e_R</m> denote the standard basis vectors of
      <m>\mathbb{R}^{R+1}</m>, indexed by zone labels rather than by the usual
      <m>1,\dots,R+1</m>. Then the <m>j</m>-th column of <m>X(s)</m> is
      <m>e_{s(j)}</m>, and the whole matrix may be written as
      <md>
        X(s)
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          e_{s(1)} \amp e_{s(2)} \amp \cdots \amp e_{s(P)}
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <example>
      <title>Membership Matrix</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose the current state is <m>s=(1,0,3,2)</m>. Then
          <md>
            X(s)
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              e_{1} \amp e_{0} \amp e_{3} \amp e_{2}
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
              1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          Reading by rows, the tray contains card <m>2</m>, the Triangle slot
          contains card <m>1</m>, the Quadrilateral slot contains card <m>4</m>,
          and the Pentagon slot contains card <m>3</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-initial-and-key-states">
    <title>Initial and Key States</title>

    <p>
      Two states are distinguished before a student acts. The <term>initial
      state</term> is the state in which every card is in the tray. In function
      form,
      <md xml:id="an-initial-state-function">
        s_0(j)=0
        \qquad
        \text{for every } j\in\mathcal{P}.
      </md>
      In vector form,
      <md>
        \mathbf{s}_0=(0,0,\dots,0).
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      To write the initial state in matrix form, let <m>\mathbf{1}_P</m> denote
      the all-ones vector of length <m>P</m>. Since <m>e_0</m> is the tray basis
      vector, the initial membership matrix is
      <md xml:id="an-initial-state-matrix">
        X_0=X(s_0)=e_0\mathbf{1}^{\top}_P.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The <term>key state</term> is the correct state determined by the answer
      key. In function form, it is
      <md xml:id="an-key-state-function">
        s^\star:\mathcal{P}\to\mathcal{Z},
      </md>
      where <m>s^\star(j)</m> is the <xref ref="gt-homezone" text="custom">home
      zone</xref> of card <m>j</m>. In matrix form, the
      <xref ref="gt-key-state" text="custom">key state</xref> is
      <md>
        X^\star=X(s^\star).
      </md>
    </p>

    <example>
      <title>Initial and Key States</title>
      <p>
        The <xref ref="gt-initial-state" text="custom">initial state</xref> for
        the running example is
        <md>
          s_0=(0,0,0,0),
        </md>
        so all four cards are in the tray. The corresponding membership matrix is
        <md xml:id="an-running-initial-matrix">
          X_0
          =
          e_0\mathbf{1}^{\top}_P
          =
          \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\0\\0\\0
          \end{bmatrix}
          \begin{bmatrix}
            1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1
          \end{bmatrix}
          =
          \begin{bmatrix}
            1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
          \end{bmatrix}.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        The key configuration is
        <md>
          s^\star=(1,1,2,0),
        </md>
        which corresponds to
        <md xml:id="an-running-key-matrix">
          X^\star =
          \begin{bmatrix}
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
            1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
          \end{bmatrix}.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        The decoy structure is visible in <m>X^\star</m>. A decoy card has its
        <m>1</m> in the tray row of the key matrix, and a decoy slot appears as
        a zero row.
      </p>
    </example>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-valid-states-configuration-space">
    <title>Valid State Spaces and Bijection</title>

    <p>
      A configuration is valid when every card occupies exactly one zone. In
      function form, validity is built into the definition
      <md>
        s:\mathcal{P}\to\mathcal{Z}.
      </md>
      The set of valid configurations is the configuration space
      <md xml:id="an-configuration-space">
        \mathcal{C}
        =
        \{s:\mathcal{P}\to\mathcal{Z}\}
        =
        \mathcal{Z}^{\mathcal{P}},
        \qquad
        |\mathcal{C}|=(R+1)^P.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      In matrix form, validity means that each column of the membership matrix
      has exactly one <m>1</m>. Let <m>\mathbf{1}_{R+1}</m> denote the all-ones
      vector of length <m>R+1</m>. Then validity is equivalently
      <md xml:id="an-valid-membership-condition">
        \mathbf{1}^{\top}_{R+1}X=\mathbf{1}^{\top}_P.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The corresponding matrix state space is
      <md xml:id="an-valid-membership-space">
        \mathcal{X}
        =
        \{X\in\{0,1\}^{(R+1)\times P}:
        \mathbf{1}^{\top}_{R+1}X=\mathbf{1}^{\top}_P\}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The spaces <m>\mathcal{C}</m> and <m>\mathcal{X}</m> contain the same
      information in different forms. Given <m>s\in\mathcal{C}</m>, the matrix
      <m>X(s)</m> places a single <m>1</m> in row <m>s(j)</m> of column <m>j</m>.
      Conversely, given <m>X\in\mathcal{X}</m>, the row containing the unique
      <m>1</m> in column <m>j</m> determines <m>s(j)</m>. Therefore
      <md>
        X:\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{X},
        \qquad
        s\mapsto X(s),
      </md>
      is a bijection.
    </p>

    <p>
      We move between these representations according to the task. The vector
      form is compact. The matrix form is convenient for row sums, occupancy,
      distance calculations, and later cohort averages. Both describe the same
      board state.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-occupancy-and-rank">
    <title>Occupancy and Occupied Zones</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The membership matrix also gives simple summaries of how cards are
        distributed across zones. These summaries describe the shape of a board
        state; they are not, by themselves, correctness scores.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-occupancy-vector-rewrite">
      <title>Occupancy</title>

      <p>
        The <term>occupancy vector</term> of a configuration is the vector of row
        sums
        <md xml:id="an-occupancy-vector">
          \mathbf{o}(X)=X\mathbf{1}_P.
        </md>
        Its <m>i</m>-th entry, denoted <m>o_i(X)</m>, counts how many cards are
        currently in zone <m>z_i</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        The total number of cards is fixed:
        <md>
          \mathbf{1}^{\top}_{R+1}X\mathbf{1}_P=P.
        </md>
        Column sums are always equal to one, because each card occupies exactly
        one zone. Row sums vary with the configuration, because zones may contain
        any number of cards, including none.
      </p>

      <p>
        The key occupancy vector <m>\mathbf{o}^\star</m> was defined at the
        design level from <m>s^\star</m>. In the matrix representation it is
        simply the row-sum vector of the key matrix:
        <md>
          \mathbf{o}^\star=X^\star\mathbf{1}_P.
        </md>
        Comparing <m>\mathbf{o}(X)</m> with <m>\mathbf{o}^\star</m> gives a
        coarse structural summary of a response: it tells how many cards are in
        each zone, but not which particular cards are there.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-occupied-zones-rank">
      <title>Occupied Zones and Rank</title>

      <p>
        The occupancy vector identifies which zones are occupied. Define the
        number of <xref ref="gt-occupied-zone" text="custom">occupied zones</xref> by
        <md>
          q(X)=\#\{i\in\mathcal{Z}:o_i(X)>0\}.
        </md>
        This quantity measures how spread out the cards are across the board. It
        is not a correctness measure: two configurations can occupy the same
        number of zones while differing completely in their relationship to the
        key.
      </p>

      <p>
        For a valid membership matrix, the occupied-zone count has a simple
        algebraic interpretation. Each column of <m>X</m> is a standard basis
        vector, so the nonzero row directions are exactly the occupied zones.
        Therefore
        <md>
          \operatorname{rank}(X)=q(X).
        </md>
      </p>

      <example>
        <title>Rank and Occupied Zones</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The <xref ref="an-running-initial-matrix" text="custom">initial
            state</xref> has rank <m>1</m> because only the tray is occupied.
            The <xref ref="an-running-key-matrix" text="custom">key state</xref>
            has rank <m>3</m>, because the tray, Triangle slot, and
            Quadrilateral slot are occupied. The incorrect state
            <m>s=(1,1,2,3)</m> also has rank <m>3</m>, but it occupies
            Triangle, Quadrilateral, and Pentagon instead.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-distance">
    <title>Distance</title>

    <p>
      The configuration space has a natural distance. Given two configurations
      <m>s,r\in\mathcal{C}</m>, define
      <md xml:id="an-state-distance-function">
        d_{\mathcal{C}}(s,r)
        =
        \#\{j\in\mathcal{P}:s(j)\neq r(j)\}.
      </md>
      This is the number of cards assigned to different zones in the two states,
      namely the Hamming distance on <m>\mathcal{Z}^{\mathcal{P}}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The same distance can be written in matrix form. If <m>X=X(s)</m> and
      <m>Y=X(r)</m>, define
      <md xml:id="an-state-distance-matrix">
        d_{\mathcal{X}}(X,Y)
        =
        \frac{1}{2}\|X-Y\|_F^2.
      </md>
      The factor of <m>1/2</m> appears because moving one card from one zone to
      another changes two entries in that card's column: one entry changes from
      <m>1</m> to <m>0</m>, and another changes from <m>0</m> to <m>1</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The two distance functions agree under the bijection between
      <m>\mathcal{C}</m> and <m>\mathcal{X}</m>. If <m>X=X(s)</m> and
      <m>Y=X(r)</m>, then
      <md>
        d_{\mathcal{C}}(s,r)=d_{\mathcal{X}}(X,Y).
      </md>
    </p>

    <example>
      <title>State Distance</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The state <m>s=(1,1,2,3)</m> differs from the key
          <m>s^\star=(1,1,2,0)</m> only in the fourth coordinate. Therefore, with
          <m>X=X(s)</m>,
          <md>
            d_{\mathcal{C}}(s,s^\star)
            =
            d_{\mathcal{X}}(X,X^\star)
            =1.
          </md>
          The circle is the only card not in its home zone.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <p>
      In this chapter, distance is only a property of pairs of states. The next
      chapter uses it to describe how states change over time, and the later
      feature chapter specializes it to comparisons with the key.
    </p>
  </section>
</chapter>
